---
import DocsLayout from "../../../components/DocsLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const criterion = `{
  "notifications": {
    "criteria": [{
      "id": "task.reminder",
      "event": {
        "id": "mdbase.runtime.timer.fired",
        "version": "1.0.0",
        "digest": "sha256:41105be7a7abf33b31ced47e1e1965242236e40ccaea286b959b0a8c591f5642"
      },
      "presentation": {
        "title": "Task reminder",
        "body": "Open Worklog to see what’s due.",
        "tag": "task-reminder"
      }
    }]
  }
}`;

const timers = `const timers = connection.timers;
if (!timers) throw new Error("This connection has no timer access");

// Replace this app’s whole set of reminders in one call.
const result = await timers.reconcile({
  namespace: "task-reminders",
  criterionId: "task.reminder",
  timers: reminders.map((r) => ({
    id: r.timerId,          // opaque: don’t put record IDs or titles here
    fireAt: r.at.toISOString(),
  })),
});
console.log(result.cancelledIds);`;

const browser = `const worker = await navigator.serviceWorker.register("/service-worker.js");

await connection.timers?.registerWebPush({
  serviceWorker: worker,
  criteria: ["task.reminder"],
});`;

const webhook = `import { verifyNotificationWebhook } from "@mdbase-dev/connect-webhooks";

const event = verifyNotificationWebhook({
  body: rawRequestBody,          // the exact bytes, before JSON parsing
  headers: request.headers,
  keys: cachedSigningKeys,
});

if (await deliveries.claim(event.delivery_id)) {
  await sendPush(event.connection_id, event.notification);
}`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Notifications and timers"
  description="Schedule reminders that fire even when every device is off. mdbase learns when they fire, never what they are about."
  active="notifications"
  headings={[
    { href: "#criteria", label: "Declare notifications" },
    { href: "#timers", label: "Schedule timers" },
    { href: "#browser", label: "Browser push" },
    { href: "#webhooks", label: "Signed webhooks" },
    { href: "#privacy", label: "What mdbase sees" }
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Timers live with mdbase, not in the collection. They fire whether or not
    any of the user’s devices are on, for Cloud copy and Private collections
    alike. When a timer fires, mdbase sends a notification with the fixed text
    from your manifest.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Timer access comes from the app connection.</strong>
    <p>
      <code>connection.timers</code> is part of <code>MdbaseConnect</code>,
      which is not available yet. See
      {" "}<a href="/sdk/connect-quickstart/">Connect a browser app</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="criteria">Declare notifications in the manifest</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={criterion} language="json" label="Manifest fragment" />
  <p>
    Timer notifications use the event <code>mdbase.runtime.timer.fired</code>
    {" "}at version <code>1.0.0</code>. The title (up to 80 characters) and body
    (up to 160) are fixed in the manifest, so no record content can reach a
    notification. You also need the <code>background.schedule</code>
    capability.
  </p>

  <h2 id="timers">Schedule timers</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={timers} language="ts" label="reminders.ts" />
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>reconcile()</code> replaces the active timers in a namespace in one
      step. Unchanged timers are kept. Changed ones are rescheduled. Ones you
      leave out are cancelled and returned in <code>cancelledIds</code>. An
      empty list cancels the namespace.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>put()</code>, <code>cancel()</code> and <code>list()</code> handle
      one timer at a time.
    </li>
    <li>
      Times are UTC instants. There is no recurrence: work out local times on
      the device and reconcile again when they change.
    </li>
    <li>
      A timer that is due while mdbase is catching up fires once, late.
    </li>
    <li>
      Namespaces are 1–64 characters, IDs 1–128, and one reconcile holds at
      most 10,000 timers.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="browser">Register browser push from a user action</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={browser} language="ts" label="enable-notifications.ts" />
  <p>
    Native apps use <code>registerFcm(&#123; token &#125;)</code>.
    {" "}<code>unregisterWebPush()</code> and <code>unregisterFcm()</code> stop
    delivery when the user opts out.
  </p>

  <h2 id="webhooks">Signed webhooks</h2>
  <p>
    If your app sends its own pushes, declare an HTTPS webhook as
    {" "}<code>notifications.native_delivery</code> with
    {" "}<code>"mode": "webhook"</code>. Verify each delivery against the exact
    raw body before parsing it.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={webhook} language="ts" label="notification-webhook.ts" />
  <p>
    Cache the signing keys from
    {" "}<code>/v1/notifications/webhook-signing-keys</code>. Deliveries older
    than five minutes are refused. Store <code>delivery_id</code> before doing
    any work: mdbase retries until your endpoint succeeds.
  </p>

  <h2 id="privacy">What mdbase sees</h2>
  <p>
    mdbase stores each timer’s namespace, ID, criterion and time, under your
    app’s grant. It learns when reminders are due and how many there are. It
    never sees record content or record IDs, unless you put them in timer IDs.
    Use opaque IDs, such as a hash.
  </p>
  <p>
    In a Private collection, timers need a grant the user has approved on one
    of their devices, with <code>background.schedule</code> kept. Opening the
    app from a notification still needs one of their devices to be online.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
